WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.869
Prediction markets offer a useful snapshot.

2
00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:06.993
But when a researcher shares a percentage, what exactly was observed?

3
00:00:06.993 --> 00:00:09.192
Which market conditions applied?

4
00:00:09.192 --> 00:00:10.896
When was the quote read?

5
00:00:10.896 --> 00:00:14.500
And does the available history really support a claim about movement?

6
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:18.508
EventScope turns those questions into a practical research workflow.

7
00:00:18.508 --> 00:00:20.058
Follow the event.

8
00:00:20.058 --> 00:00:21.677
Keep the evidence.

9
00:00:21.677 --> 00:00:25.216
Start by discovering an event through Panta's market catalogue.

10
00:00:25.216 --> 00:00:29.549
Inspect its YES and NO quotes, conditions and recent trade records.

11
00:00:29.549 --> 00:00:33.948
Watch up to four events while the browser is open, and compare up to three.

12
00:00:33.948 --> 00:00:36.142
Then capture an Evidence Brief.

13
00:00:36.142 --> 00:00:43.425
Markdown, JSON and CSV exports preserve the same market, quote, source mode and actual read timestamp.

14
00:00:43.425 --> 00:00:46.409
The difference is how EventScope treats uncertainty.

15
00:00:46.409 --> 00:00:49.013
An unavailable quote stays unavailable.

16
00:00:49.013 --> 00:00:53.402
Charts contain actual detail reads rather than invented historical prices.

17
00:00:53.402 --> 00:00:57.046
Phase changes and observation gaps interrupt continuity.

18
00:00:57.046 --> 00:01:04.124
A brief preserves these limits alongside the evidence, helping researchers explain which conclusions their observations can support.

19
00:01:04.124 --> 00:01:06.573
Panta is central to the live product.

20
00:01:06.573 --> 00:01:12.841
Four read resources supply categories, market discovery, detail quotes and a bounded trade tape.

21
00:01:12.841 --> 00:01:15.025
Credentials remain on the server.

22
00:01:15.025 --> 00:01:20.419
Caching, request coalescing, timeouts and rate-limit backoff keep reads controlled.

23
00:01:20.419 --> 00:01:25.527
The homepage defaults to live data once a deployer configures a legitimate Panta API key.

24
00:01:25.527 --> 00:01:34.090
The open-source prototype has been checked against actual Panta production responses, including available quotes, automatic refresh and a live-source export.

25
00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:38.618
The repository separates those dated checks from fictional walkthrough examples.

26
00:01:38.618 --> 00:01:46.451
Verification also includes one hundred and ten unit tests, twenty-three browser workflows, TypeScript checks and a production build.

27
00:01:46.451 --> 00:01:49.755
Monitoring currently runs while the browser remains open.

28
00:01:49.755 --> 00:01:53.504
The initial audience is independent event researchers and creators.

29
00:01:53.504 --> 00:01:59.557
The next validation step would be to observe whether a timestamped brief saves effort when they cite prediction-market data.

30
00:01:59.557 --> 00:02:04.496
Repeat use and reuse of exported evidence would be more useful signals than a one-time visit.

31
00:02:04.496 --> 00:02:07.919
No external adoption or revenue has yet been established.

32
00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:16.567
If that demand is demonstrated, shared team workspaces and reusable evidence feeds could be tested as paid services, subject to Panta's commercial terms.

33
00:02:16.567 --> 00:02:18.911
These are future hypotheses.

34
00:02:18.911 --> 00:02:27.029
Today's deliverable is a working, responsive research prototype with source code and integration evidence, independently authored with AI assistance.

35
00:02:27.029 --> 00:02:32.132
EventScope makes each observation something a researcher can keep, inspect and explain.
